g the file, and \"options_map\", a dict of the\n   125→      options parsed from the file.\n   126→    \"\"\"\n   127→    contents = encryption.read_encrypted_file(filename)\n   128→    return load_string(contents,\n   129→                       log_timings=log_timings,\n   130→                       log_errors=log_errors,\n   131→                       extra_validations=extra_validations,\n   132→                       encoding=encoding)\n   133→\n   134→\n   135→def _log_errors(errors, log_errors):\n   136→    \"\"\"Log errors, if 'log_errors' is set.\n   137→\n   138    Args:\n   139→      log_errors: A file object or function to write errors to,\n   140→        or None, if it should remain quiet.\n   141→    \"\"\"\n   142→    if log_errors and errors:\n   143→        if hasattr(log_errors, 'write'):\n   144→            printer.print_errors(errors, file=log_errors)\n   145→        else:\n   146→            error_io = io.StringIO()\n   147→            printer.print_errors(errors, file=error_io)\n   148→            log_errors(error_io.getvalue())\n   149→\n   150→\n   151→def get_cache_filename(pattern: str, filename: str) -> str:\n   152→    \"\"\"Compute the cache filename from a given pattern and the top-level filename.\n   153→\n   154→    Args:\n   155→      pattern: A cache filename or pattern. If the pattern contains '{filename}' this\n   156→        will get replaced by the top-level filename. This may be absolute or relative.\n   157→      filename: The top-level filename.\n   158→    Returns:\n   159→      The resolved cache filename.\n   160→    \"\"\"\n   161→    abs_filename = path.abspath(filename)\n   162→    if path.isabs(pattern):\n   163→        abs_pattern = pattern\n   164→    else:\n   165→        abs_pattern = path.join(path.dirname(abs_filename), pattern)\n   166→    return abs_pattern.format(filename=path.basename(filename))\n   167→\n   168→\n   169→def pickle_cache_function(cache_getter, time_threshold, function):\n   170→    \"\"\"Decorate a loader function to make it loads its result from a pickle cache.\n   171→\n   172→    This considers the first argument as a top-level filename and assumes the\n   173→    function to be cached returns an (entries, errors, options_map) triple. We\n   174→    use the 'include' option value in order to check whether any of the included\n   175→    files has changed. It's essentially a special case for an on-disk memoizer.\n   176→    If any of the included files are more recent than the cache, the function is\n   177→    recomputed and the cache refreshed.\n   178→\n   179→    Args:\n   180→      cache_getter: A function of one argument, the top-level filename, which\n   181→        will return the name of the corresponding cache file.\n   182→      time_threshold: A float, the number of seconds below which we don't bother\n   183→        caching.\n   184→      function: A function object to decorate for caching.\n   185→    Returns:\n   186→      A decorated function which will pull its result from a cache file if\n   187→      it is available.\n   188→    \"\"\"\n   189→    @functools.wraps(function)\n   190→    def wrapped(toplevel_filename, *args, **kw):\n   191→        cache_filename = cache_getter(toplevel_filename)\n   192→\n   193→        # Read the cache if it exists in order to get the list of files whose\n   194→        # timestamps to check.\n   195→        exists = path.exists(cache_filename)\n   196→        if exists:\n   197→            with open(cache_filename, 'rb') as file:\n   198→                try:\n   199→                    result = pickle.load(file)\n   200→                except Exception as exc:\n   201→                    # Note: Not a big fan of doing this, but here we handle all\n   202→                    # possible exceptions because unpickling of an old or\n   203→                    # corrupted pickle file manifests as a variety of different\n   204→                    # exception types.\n   205→\n   206→                    # The